# bug-nilpy-a-class-used-as-a-value-segfaults-or-refuses
#
# A class used as an ordinary VALUE — bound to a name, put in a list or dict,
# passed as an argument — used to COMPILE CLEAN and then SEGFAULT:
#
#     for cls in [A]:
#         x = cls(3)        # SIGSEGV
#
# The class travelled through the container as its untagged RTTI-blob address,
# so boxing it into a variant made it an ordinary INTEGER (`print(cls)` showed a
# bare number where CPython shows `<class '__main__.A'>`) and calling it jumped
# to the blob. A segfault from ordinary valid Python is the worst outcome
# available, so it is now REFUSED BY NAME until class references are real values.
#
# Measured before refusing, because a blanket refusal would have been wrong:
# `isinstance(x, A)`, `except A:` and `A(...)` construction each have their own
# intercept and never reach this site, so none of them pays for it. That is
# asserted positively in test_nilpy_instance_named_like_its_class.npy and the
# other class tests, which must keep compiling.
#
# This file pins the REFUSAL. When class references become first-class values
# (which needs a distinct variant tag — the same thing
# bug-nilpy-calling-a-non-callable-segfaults needs), this test must be RETIRED,
# exactly as implementing starred unpacking retired its two refusal tests.
# Making it merely PARSE without making it correct would resurrect a segfault.
class A:
    def __init__(self, v):
        self.v = v


for cls in [A]:
    x = cls(3)
    print(x.v)
